# One line ending everywhere, LF, in the working tree as much as in the object
# store. A Windows checkout defaulting to CRLF broke two things that were hard to
# see:
#
#   * `sh install.sh` from a Windows clone reads a CRLF script and dies on
#     `$'\r': command not found`.
#   * A build inside a Linux container marked every file modified, because git
#     there compares CRLF working-tree files against LF blobs. The version stamp
#     then reported `-dirty` for a tree that was clean.
#
# Nothing in this repository wants CRLF, so there is no exception list.
* text=auto eol=lf
